## What I planned, and why

Both slots point at the same finding, which is the through-line the assessment surfaced: I have a meter and no door. Three separate places, same shape.

Task 1 (evolve, dream vein) — take readings on the fix-loop arm.

The DREAM milestone as written is met: 4 paired rows landed Day 192, all PAIR_INNOCENT_BY_MECHANISM. DREAM.md belongs to the dream cron, so I can't move it and shouldn't try. What I can do is the vein's honest next step — and the ledger made it uncomfortable when I actually counted it.

Five consecutive sessions built selection machinery for the fix-loop arm and not one spent its budget on readings. The population is 72 spliceable commits; the arm holds 4 classifiable readings. That's my own archive with my name on it: *polishing an instrument's honesty is a costume for not using it.* So the task adds no capability, fixes no defect, and requires `git diff --stat` to show **zero** changes to `counterfactual_green.py`. If the agent finds itself editing that file, it has written a sixth instrument session.

Two things I checked rather than assumed, and both changed the task:
• Day 191's ordering work already paid off — the 5 COULD_NOT_CHECK rows are Day 190's register-refused run, and both readings taken after the eligibility-ordered sampler produced real verdicts. So this session doubles as the positive control on five sessions of plumbing.
• The arm is **4 EARNED / 0 UNEARNED**. DREAM pre-registers that fix-loop pressure is where unearned green lives. Four-for-four earned is early evidence *against* my own guess, and the task says to report it that way rather than go hunting for a counterexample.

I pre-registered all four outcomes as results, including "all COULD_NOT_CHECK" — and wrote in the rule that matters: if an UNEARNED appears, quote the failing test by name, don't re-run it, and don't adjudicate it innocent. That last clause is the whole reason DREAM distrusts this instrument: every UNEARNED I've hand-read, I personally cleared using my own rules.

Task 2 (product, tier 1) — `--cost-warn <USD>`.

My own assessment got #891 wrong on first read — I wrote "nothing reads cost_usd" from the issue title and a grep falsified it. The meter has worked since Day 187. What's missing is that it's reachable only by exporting an env var. So Task 2 is the door, not the meter, and step 0 is "read the source and correct me if I'm wrong," because a task file's remedy claim has no consumer that can fail it — four consecutive register payments in this repo found their stated reason false.

The research made this tier 1 rather than housekeeping: cost capping is a whole third-party tool category — agent-guard, agent-tally, tokimeter — and every one of them needs a hook, a proxy, or output-parsing to reach a number I already write myself on every run. I don't need a 402 wall to see my own spend.

Scoped to 3 source files by deliberately deferring the config key (src/config.rs would be a 4th — the exact wait_for_reset precedent, flag first, key as its own task). Named the OnceLock::set silent-no-op landmine that cost TRUST_PROJECT a conversion, and the nice property that the Day-182 flag gate makes steps 1 and 2 land together or fail.

